---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";

// Issue #1105: "Export" — JSON, CSV, FHIR, and the clinician PDF, plus the
// guardian-lens rule for private notes. Every claim carries a `cite:`
// comment and a site/claims.md entry (checked by
// test/site/site_claims_test.dart).

const description =
  "Everything logged in lunarlog comes back out: a JSON archive, two CSV " +
  "spreadsheets, a FHIR R4 summary for clinicians, and a one-page PDF — " +
  "built on your device, no account required.";
---

<BaseLayout title="Export" description={description}>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Export</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      Your data is yours to take, in the format you need. Settings → Your
      data builds each file on your device and hands it to your device's
      share sheet — you choose where it goes. No account is required.
    </p>

    <figure class="screenshot-figure">
      <Screenshot
        screen="export"
        device="iphone-67"
        theme="light"
        loading="eager"
        alt="Settings — Your data, with the export and restore entry points."
      />
      <figcaption>Settings → Your data, where every export lives.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2>The four exports</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Export Your Data" (JSON; built from the on-device copy; works fully offline and without a cloud account). -->
      <dl>
        <dt><strong>Export my data</strong> (JSON)</dt>
        <dd>
          Everything on this device — every profile and entry you can see,
          with each entry's author attribution. Built entirely from the
          device copy, so it works fully offline, with no account.
        </dd>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Export as CSV" (cycles + daily_log; notes and tags as columns; formula-injection escaping). -->
        <dt><strong>Export as CSV</strong></dt>
        <dd>
          Two spreadsheet-friendly tables — one row per cycle, one per day
          — with notes and tags in their own columns. Text that could be
          read as a spreadsheet formula is escaped, so a CSV opens as data,
          never as something a spreadsheet executes.
        </dd>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Export a Clinical Summary (FHIR)" (FHIR R4; LOINC/SNOMED CT where a verified mapping exists, never a guessed code; self-reported labelling; ISP-shaped without claiming compliance; display name only). -->
        <dt><strong>Export clinical summary</strong> (FHIR)</dt>
        <dd>
          One profile's history as a FHIR R4 bundle some patient portals can
          import directly. Clinical codes are used where a verified mapping
          exists and lunarlog's own codes elsewhere — never a guessed code —
          and the file says throughout that the data is self-reported, not a
          clinician's assessment. It carries only the profile's display
          name: no email, no account id.
        </dd>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Export a Clinical Summary (PDF)" (one page, on-device, display name only, never note text); lib/domain/export/clinical_pdf.dart. -->
        <dt><strong>Export clinical summary</strong> (PDF)</dt>
        <dd>
          A one-page, on-device summary for an appointment: the profile's
          cycle and symptom history, its display name only, and no note
          text at all.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>You pick the destination</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §4 "Clinical export is a user-initiated transfer, not collection" (built on-device, handed to the share sheet; a transfer you control). -->
      <p>
        No export is uploaded anywhere by lunarlog. Each file is built on
        your device and handed to your share sheet — AirDrop, Files, email,
        or any app you choose. Handing one to a clinician is your transfer,
        between you and them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a guardian's export leaves out</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Export Your Data" (guardian-lens rule: a note the subject marked private is omitted from JSON and CSV; FHIR and PDF never include note text at all). -->
      <p>
        If you export a profile you share but the profile's subject marked a
        day note private, that note is not in your file — the same rule the
        app itself follows. The subject's own export carries their private
        notes in full.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>And it goes back in</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/domain/import/account_import.dart / lib/data/import/account_importer.dart (issue #140): additive restore, nothing overwritten or deleted. -->
      <p>
        A JSON export can be read back into lunarlog on any device — an
        additive restore that adds what is missing and overwrites nothing.
        See <a href="/import">Import</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      Exports are one half of owning your data; the other half is
      <a href="/delete-account">deleting it</a>, in the app, any time.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
